{
  "level": "b2",
  "target": "de",
  "grammarSchedule": [
    {
      "id": "de.b2.concessive-clauses",
      "fromLesson": 1,
      "why": "obwohl is A2 and trotzdem is B1, so both halves of the topic are already in hand; what B2 adds is dennoch, which lesson 1 teaches as `even so`. The whole point of the topic is that the two constructions take different word orders, and a lesson that hands over a third connector is the place to show all three side by side"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.free-relative-clauses",
      "fromLesson": 3,
      "why": "wer, was and wo are A1 question words and the construction costs no new vocabulary at all. It waits for lesson 3 because a free relative needs something worth generalising about - `wer etwas behauptet, muss es belegen` - and lesson 3 is where the verbs of asserting arrive"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.modal-particles",
      "fromLesson": 5,
      "why": "doch, ja, eben, denn and schon are all A1 or A2 words carrying an unrelated dictionary meaning, so nothing has to be taught first. Lesson 5 is early enough that the particles can then colour sentences for the remaining 75 lessons, which is the only way a learner ever gets a feel for them"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.konjunktiv-i-reported-speech",
      "fromLesson": 8,
      "why": "reported speech needs someone to be reported. Lessons 3 and 4 supply behaupten, argumentieren, betonen and andeuten, and lesson 8 adds the verbs that carry a report forward. The forms themselves - sei, habe, könne, werde - belong to A1 verbs"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.nominalization",
      "fromLesson": 10,
      "why": "the construction is only visible when the noun and the verb it was built from are both known. Lesson 10 opens the abstract-noun run: die Annahme beside annehmen, die Folge beside folgen, die Voraussetzung beside voraussetzen"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.konjunktiv-i-substitute-form",
      "fromLesson": 12,
      "why": "this is a repair rule for the previous topic, not a separate construction, so it has to follow it far enough that Konjunktiv I is familiar. Four lessons is that distance; the plural forms it exists to rescue turn up as soon as a report has more than one speaker"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.irreal-comparison-als-ob",
      "fromLesson": 14,
      "why": "als ob is a B1 word and Konjunktiv II is a B1 topic, so the machinery is complete before B2 starts. It waits until lesson 14 because an unreal comparison needs an appearance worth doubting, and lessons 12 and 13 are where a claim, a statement and an interpretation become nameable"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.passive-alternatives",
      "fromLesson": 16,
      "why": "all three forms say that something can be done, so the topic needs problems that can. Lessons 15 and 16 give risk, challenge and requirement, and the -bar half rests on vergleichbar from B1 until machbar arrives in lesson 20"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.participle-i-as-adjective",
      "fromLesson": 18,
      "why": "a Partizip I is a verb stem plus -d plus an adjective ending, so it costs no new word; it costs a noun worth modifying. Lesson 18 is where the adjective run starts, which is where a learner is already reading endings"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.fixed-prepositions-adjectives-nouns",
      "fromLesson": 22,
      "why": "stolz auf is A2, die Angst vor and der Grund für are B1, so the pattern can be shown from stock. Lesson 22 places it beside the softening adverbs, and B2 keeps feeding it later: anfällig für in lesson 35, angewiesen sein auf in the working-hours run"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.temporal-connectors-extended",
      "fromLesson": 24,
      "why": "sobald, solange and indem are all B1 words that B1 never scheduled a topic for. Lesson 24 has the reporting vocabulary that makes a means-clause worth writing - er belegte es, indem er die Umfrage zitierte"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.support-verb-constructions",
      "fromLesson": 28,
      "why": "a Funktionsverbgefüge is a fixed noun-plus-light-verb pair, so it needs the noun. in Frage stellen rests on A2 die Frage and zum Abschluss kommen on A2 der Abschluss, but the register only makes sense once lessons 25 to 28 have established that this is how reports are written"
    },
    {
      "id": "de.b2.extended-participial-attribute",
      "fromLesson": 34,
      "why": "the hardest construction at this level: a participle carrying its own objects and adverbs, all before the noun. It waits for lesson 34 so that Partizip I has had sixteen lessons to settle and so that the modifiers being packed in - a date, an agent, a place - are words the learner reads without effort"
    }
  ],
